CONTRIBUTIONS. A pull request is an offer of code to an unlicensed project.
Until a licence is chosen, contributors are asked to confirm in the pull
request that they hold the rights to what they submit and that the copyright
holder may relicense it under whatever licence this repository adopts. This is
recorded in .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md.

This file previously carried an MIT license. That was replaced on 2026-08-18
because MIT permits anyone -- including a competitor -- to take this code and
operate it as a commercial service, which is incompatible with the intended
direction of this project. Removing it preserves every option: adding a
permissive license later is easy, while withdrawing one already published is
not.

WHEN A LICENSE IS CHOSEN, two are designed for this exact situation and are
worth reading before defaulting to MIT or Apache-2.0:

  AGPL-3.0  Copyleft that extends to NETWORK use. A third party may run it,
            but must publish their modified source. Closes the "operate it as
            a closed SaaS" gap that MIT leaves wide open.

  BSL 1.1   Business Source License. Source-available with a time delay:
            restricted commercial use for a fixed term (commonly 3-4 years),
            then converts automatically to an open license you nominate.

A dual arrangement -- AGPL-3.0 for the public, a separate commercial license
for paying customers -- is the usual pattern for an open-core product.

This is not legal advice. Obtain counsel before publishing under any license,
and before any commercial use.

===============================================================================
SCOPE AND DOWNSTREAM CONSTRAINTS — PLEASE READ
===============================================================================

Whatever license is eventually chosen for THE CODE, it cannot grant rights over
the model weights, the training data, or the fonts this software generates.
Three separate constraints apply downstream, and the decision above does not
affect any of them:

1. BASE MODEL — FLUX.2-klein-base-9B is distributed under the FLUX
   Non-Commercial License. Commercial use of that model is NOT permitted.
   The Apache-2.0 FLUX.2-klein-4B line is a permissive alternative but would
   require retraining the adapter.
   See research/2026-07-27-klein-licensing-and-4b-port.md

2. TRAINING DATA — the corpus is ~87% SIL Open Font License fonts (NOT the
   97.5% previously stated here; see research/2026-08-08-the-corpus-is-not-
   97-percent-ofl.md). 49 files (~5%) carry vendor-supplied terms that permit
   rendering content but not redistribution or conversion, and ~5% more has
   provenance that could not be established. That is an unresolved
   right-to-train question separate from, and not cured by, the OFL terms
   below. SIL's
   OFL FAQ 1.25 (Nov 2023) states that a font produced by a system whose
   training data contains OFL sources "should be considered a derivative
   work," and must itself be licensed under the OFL. Enforceability is
   genuinely unsettled and no case law exists.
   See research/2026-07-27-ofl-derivative-work-constraint.md

3. GENERATED FONTS — per (2), fonts produced by this software should be
   treated as OFL derivatives. OFL permits commercial use and modification,
   but requires the OFL license accompany them and forbids selling the font
   software on its own.

None of the above is legal advice. Obtain counsel before any commercial use.
